  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 215 AM MDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nCurrent conditions show a dramatic difference from this time last\nnight, with overnight temperatures a good 15 to 25 degrees cooler\nthan this time last night. Today's high temperatures are still \nexpected to struggle to make it out of the 70s across our lower \nelevations. This cooler airmass didn't have enough oomph to make \nmuch of a difference in our mountain valleys, where afternoon \nhighs are expected to warm into the mid to upper 80s once again\ntoday. With increasing zonal flow aloft expected to bring some \ngusty winds (25-30 mph) into North and Middle Park this afternoon,\nwhere much drier air still resides, a Red Flag Warning is in \neffect from 1 PM to 7 PM for these locations as relative \nhumidities are expected to drop into the low to mid teens. \nPrecipitation chances will remain limited to the southern Front \nRange and Palmer Divide this afternoon, where a few showers and \nthunderstorms will be possible. Westerly flow aloft will keep\nsmoke around, with patchy smoke expected across the forecast area\nfor at least another day. \n\nWednesday will bring one more day of below normal temperatures to\nour lower elevations, though it will mark the start of another\nwarming trend, with zonal flow turning more to the northwest as \nupper-level ridging starts to rebuild across the west. Critical\nfire weather conditions are expected across our higher elevations,\nexpanding slightly due to RH dropping into the single digits\ncoinciding with gusty winds up to 30 mph. A Fire Weather Watch has\nbeen issued for our mountain valleys and elevations above 9,000'.  \n\nOur stretch of record-breaking heat appears poised to continue as\ntemperatures are expected to gradually warm each day through the \nweekend, with some locations on the plains reaching the triple- \ndigits as early as Friday. Precipitation chances don't return to \nthe forecast until late this weekend/early next week, and \nconfidence in any meaningful rainfall remains low.",
